<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Takano Chōei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Takano Choei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Takano Chōei was a Japanese physician and scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> learning (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rangaku">rangaku</a>) who studied under Philipp Franz von Siebold at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>. In 1838 he criticised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s policy of firing on foreign ships and was imprisoned in the purge of 1839. He escaped in 1844, was sheltered for a time by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-munenari">Date Munenari</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uwajima">Uwajima</a>, for whom he translated Western military works, and killed himself in 1850 when officers came to arrest him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/takano-choei.jpg" alt="Takano Chōei" width="295" height="600" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Painting: Tsubaki Chinzan (1801-1854)Reproduction: User:Iwatebud · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Takano_Choei.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
